  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 246 AM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nAs of 245 AM Wednesday morning, broad southwesterly midlevel flow \ncontinues across the Plains with lee trough developing across high \nPlains. This is promoting the strengthening of a LLJ from west TX \nthrough much of KS. Steepening midlevel lapse rates combined with \nthe WAA may result in isolated showers or thunderstorms through \nsunrise and mainly across the Flint Hills. Elevated instability up \nto 500 J/kg should limit any potential for strong or severe storms. \nA shortwave trough will slide across the northern Plains through the \nday today, shunting a surface trough towards central KS. Ahead of \nthe front, temperatures will rebound into the upper 70s to near 80. \nAdditional WAA driven thunderstorms are possible late Wednesday \nnight into Thursday morning but the best chances may stay across \nnortheast KS into northwest MO. \n\nRain chances will diminish into the daytime hours Thursday as \nshortwave ridging slides across the central Plains ahead of the next \nshortwave trough axis. This shortwave trough is posed to lift from \nthe Baja region late Thursday night into Friday. This should result \nin widespread rain showers Friday and even Sunday. \n\nMid to long range guidance continues to bring a strong midlevel \ntrough into the central Plains early next week. This could bring \nmultiple days of rainfall, strong winds, and even the potential for \nstrong or severe weather. Stay tuned.",
